<TopicPage slug="subjects/history/foundational/the-world-wars">
  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This lesson needs zero prior knowledge — no earlier history lesson, no dates
    memorised. The two wars are told here as one connected story, built step by step
    from the alliance system to the post-1945 order.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>explain how the alliance system, colonial rivalry and the Balkans turned one assassination into a general war in five weeks;</li>
    <li>describe the First World War's industrial slaughter and why the Versailles peace failed;</li>
    <li>trace the three cracks — economic collapse, fascism, appeasement — that grew into the Second World War;</li>
    <li>set out the course of 1939–45, including the Eastern Front's centrality and the Holocaust;</li>
    <li>list what the wars built: the United Nations, welfare states, decolonisation, Nuremberg and the nuclear age.</li>
  </ul>
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    The two world wars are history's hinge: <strong>the empires, ideologies, borders and
    institutions of today were cast in their wreckage</strong>. In thirty-one years — 1914 to
    1945 — four dynastic empires collapsed, mapmakers redrew the world, roughly 70 to 90 million
    people died, war became industrial and then existential, and the survivors built a new order
    (the United Nations, welfare states, the American and Soviet superpowers) designed around
    the explicit hope of never repeating it. If you understand these two wars, you understand why
    the world after 1945 is built the way it is.
  </p>
  <p>
    It is tempting to treat them as two separate disasters, but historians read them as one
    extended crisis with an armistice in the middle: the <strong>Versailles settlement</strong>
    that ended the First World War created grievances and instabilities that the Second grew in.
    This chapter therefore moves in four movements — the causes of 1914 (where the syllabus's
    analytical questions live), the first war and its failed peace, the causes and course of the
    second, and what the two built and destroyed. The widget gives the frames; the sections give
    the mechanisms.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the hinge of the twentieth century, in four frames" widget="equationStep" config={wwCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the frames tell one story, not two — frame 2, "the
    peace that failed", is the hinge, since the Second World War grew in the cracks
    Versailles left. Read frames 1 and 3 as the same mechanism run twice at larger
    scale, and frame 4 as a checklist of reactions to the whole crisis.
  </p>

  <h2>The alliance system: safety net or tripwire?</h2>
  <p>
    By 1907 Europe was divided into two fortified camps. The <strong>Triple Alliance</strong>
    (Germany, Austria-Hungary, Italy, from 1882) faced the <strong>Triple Entente</strong>
    (France and Russia, allied in 1894, joined loosely by Britain through the 1904 Entente
    Cordiale and the 1907 Anglo-Russian agreement). The stated logic was deterrence: if war
    threatened one power, its partners would restrain it, because an aggressor would face two
    fronts. Did the system make war more likely or less likely? The honest answer is
    <em>both, in different ways</em>. For forty years the balance helped keep the great-power
    peace; crises over Morocco (1905, 1911) and the Balkans were defused rather than escalated.
    But the alliance network had two structural flaws. First, it turned any local quarrel into a
    potential continental one — there was no quarrel so small that an ally might not be dragged
    in. Second, the alliances were secret or vague, so no government could be sure what its
    partners would do, and each hedged with more arms. The result was a <strong>tripolar
    arms race</strong> — the Anglo-German naval race after HMS <em>Dreadnought</em> (1906),
    and standing armies that nearly doubled in size between 1870 and 1914.
  </p>
  <Widget no="2" caption="file each fact — safety net or tripwire?" widget="sort-buckets" config={wwAllianceBuckets} />
  <p>
    The deepest problem was inside the alliances: Germany's war plan. The <strong>Schlieffen
    Plan</strong> required defeating France within about six weeks <em>before</em> turning east
    against Russia, whose army would take far longer to mobilise. That timetable meant German
    mobilisation could not be paused without forfeiting the plan — which is why, in the July
    crisis of 1914, mobilisation orders behaved like triggers. Christopher Clark's famous verdict
    calls the 1914 governments "sleepwalkers": awake but blind, "watchful but unseeing", walking
    into a war none of them had decided to start.
  </p>

  <h2>Colonial rivalry: empire as engine of tension</h2>
  <p>
    How far did colonial problems create tension? Substantially, though the mechanism was
    indirect. After 1880 the European powers carved up Africa and Asia in the <em>Scramble for
    Africa</em>; by 1914 Britain and France controlled empires covering roughly a sixth and a
    tenth of the world's land. Empire mattered for three reasons. It was <strong>prestige</strong>
    — a great power, by definition, had colonies, so a rising power like Germany felt entitled to
    its "place in the sun" and resented arriving to find the map already coloured. It was
    <strong>economy</strong> — raw materials, captive markets and investment outlets, the raw
    feedstock of pre-war protectionism. And it was <strong>friction</strong>: every new colonial
    boundary was a potential flashpoint between powers. The <strong>Morocco Crises</strong> show
    the pattern. In 1905 Germany challenged France's new protectorate over Morocco; the
    Algeciras conference (1906) humiliated Germany but pushed Britain and France into closer
    military conversation. In 1911 the German gunboat <em>Panther</em> sailed to Agadir, and
    France bought Germany off with chunks of the French Congo — while a British minister
    (Lloyd George) delivered a speech that read as a warning. Each crisis ended in compromise and
    left both sides more convinced the other was encircling them. Colonial competition did not
    cause the war of 1914 directly — the trigger was European — but it normalised rivalry and
    armed it.
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="pair each reason with its mechanism" widget="match" config={wwEmpireMatch} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> the pre-war world was not universally regarded as peaceful.
  Well-informed contemporaries expected a great war eventually — Norman Angell's 1910 bestseller
  <em>The Great Illusion</em> argued conquest had become economically pointless, and was widely
  read as proving war was inevitable <em>and</em> futile. The surprise of 1914 was the timing and
  the totality, not the possibility.</p>

  <h2>The Balkans: the powder keg explained</h2>
  <p>
    Why were Balkan problems so hard for the Great Powers to solve? Because the peninsula
    combined <em>everything dangerous at once</em>: a decaying empire (Ottoman Turkey) losing
    territory, young nationalism (Serbia, Bulgaria, Greece, Romania) expanding into the vacuum,
    and every great power with a stake in the outcome. Russia claimed to protect fellow Slavs and
    Orthodox Christians — above all <strong>Serbia</strong>; Austria-Hungary, itself a patchwork
    of eleven nationalities, saw a successful South-Slav nationalism as an existential threat
    that would unpick its own empire; Germany backed Vienna ("blank cheque" loyalty); Britain
    and France wanted the Ottomans propped up as a buffer against Russia. Two Balkan Wars
    (1912–13) had already driven the Ottomans almost out of Europe and doubled Serbia, leaving
    Austria-Hungary convinced Serbia must be broken before it was too late.
  </p>
  <p>
    Into this walked the assassination. On <strong>28 June 1914</strong>, Archduke Franz
    Ferdinand — heir to the Austro-Hungarian throne — was shot in Sarajevo by <strong>Gavrilo
    Princip</strong>, a 19-year-old Bosnian Serb trained and armed by <em>Black Hand</em>
    nationalists with links into Serbian army intelligence. Austria-Hungary, with Germany's
    unconditional support, issued Serbia an ultimatum on 23 July whose terms were designed to be
    refused; Serbia accepted almost everything but not all; Austria declared war on 28 July.
    Russia then mobilised to defend Serbia; Germany declared war on Russia (1 August) and France
    (3 August), and invaded Belgium, bringing Britain in on 4 August. From one murder to a world
    war in five weeks — each step locally "logical", collectively catastrophic. That chain is
    the answer to the syllabus question: the alliance system converted a Balkan duel into a
    general war, and mobilisation timetables removed the last room for hesitation.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="line up July–August 1914, earliest first" widget="order" config={wwChainOrder} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> the assassination in Sarajevo caused the First World War. It caused the <em>timing</em>: forty years of alliance architecture, an arms race and mobilisation timetables that removed hesitation made a general war probable, and the murder picked the week. Remove Princip and Europe still holds a powder keg; keep Princip and remove the tripwires, and July 1914 is another Balkan crisis.</p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the assassination caused the timing, not the war — forty
    years of alliance architecture, an arms race and mobilisation timetables that removed
    hesitation turned one Balkan murder into a general war in five weeks.
  </p>

  <h2>The First World War and the peace that failed</h2>
  <p>
    The war everyone expected to be over by Christmas became four years of industrial
    slaughter. New weapons — machine guns, heavy artillery, poison gas, tanks, aircraft — met
    nineteenth-century tactics, and the Western Front congealed into trenches from Switzerland
    to the Channel. The Somme's first day (1 July 1916) cost Britain 57,000 casualties; the
    battle eventually killed or wounded over a million men for a few miles. Around
    <strong>10 million soldiers</strong> died. The war destroyed the German, Austro-Hungarian,
    Russian and Ottoman empires, pulled the United States into European affairs (1917), and
    ended on 11 November 1918.
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="call the toll — the first day of the Somme" widget="estimate" config={wwSommeEstimate} />
  <p>
    The <strong>Treaty of Versailles</strong> (June 1919) tried to solve the problem it had
    inherited and made the next one. Germany lost about 13% of its territory, all its colonies,
    its navy, and was made to accept <strong>war guilt</strong> (Article 231) as the basis for
    <strong>reparations</strong> set in 1921 at 132 billion gold marks. The economist Keynes,
    present at the conference, predicted the terms would poison European recovery. Yet Germany
    was not dismembered or permanently occupied — it was punished just enough to be enraged and
    not enough to be disabled, the worst of both worlds. The peacemakers also redrew eastern
    Europe on the principle of nationality, creating Poland, Czechoslovakia, Yugoslavia and the
    Baltic states — with millions of minorities on the "wrong" side of each new border, and a
    Germany, Soviet Russia and (later) Italy determined to revise the settlement. The League of
    Nations, the first general peace organisation, lacked the United States (whose Senate refused
    to join), and its members' will to enforce its decisions died with the Depression.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="find the faulty line — the peace that failed" widget="spot-error" config={wwVersaillesSpot} />

  <h2>The road to the Second World War</h2>
  <p>
    The second catastrophe grew in three cracks. First, <strong>economic collapse</strong>: the
    Great Depression from 1929 threw a third of German workers out of jobs and discredited
    democracy across Europe. Second, <strong>fascism</strong>: Mussolini had taken power in
    Italy in 1922; from the wreckage of Weimar Germany, <strong>Adolf Hitler</strong> and the
    Nazi Party — openly committed to overturning Versailles, destroying "Judeo-Bolshevism" in the
    east, and conquering <em>Lebensraum</em> (living space) — came to power legally in January
    1933 and remade Germany into a war state within six years, with the Nuremberg racial laws
    (1935) and Kristallnacht (1938) marking the escalation of antisemitic persecution. Third,
    <strong>appeasement</strong>: Britain and France, scarred by 1914–18 and preoccupied by
    empire and Depression, tried to satisfy the revisionists cheaply. Japan had already seized
    Manchuria (1931) and Italy Abyssinia (1935–36) while the League watched. Hitler remilitarised
    the Rhineland (1936), annexed Austria (March 1938), and at <strong>Munich</strong>
    (September 1938) Britain and France handed him the Czech Sudetenland in exchange for peace
    promises that lasted barely six months. When Germany invaded the rest of Czechoslovakia in
    March 1939, the policy finally reversed: guarantees to Poland, and war on 3 September 1939
    after Hitler invaded it on the 1st.
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="the Cabinet's dilemma, 1936–39 — decide" widget="branch" config={wwMunichBranch} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> appeasement was simple cowardice with no rationale. The policy grew from democracies scarred by 1914-18, preoccupied by empire and Depression, and unsure their rearmament could match Germany's — and the discovery that German rearmament was far from ready in 1938 keeps the "did it buy a year?" argument alive. The verdict of folly rests on what Munich taught Hitler, not on what Chamberlain knew.</p>

  <h2>The Second World War and the Holocaust</h2>
  <p>
    The 1939–45 war was deadlier and more genuinely global than the first: <strong>60 to 80
    million dead</strong>, the majority civilians. Germany conquered most of continental Europe
    by mid-1940; Britain held out alone through the Battle of Britain; and in June 1941 Hitler
    launched <strong>Barbarossa</strong> against the Soviet Union — the decision that destroyed
    him. The Eastern Front became history's largest and cruellest land war: by starvation
    policy, siege (Leningrad) and extermination, it killed <strong>roughly 27 million Soviet
    citizens</strong>, and about four out of five German soldiers who died in the whole war died
    there. Japan's attack on Pearl Harbor (7 December 1941) brought the United States in, making
    the conflict truly worldwide. The Allies' victory was built on production and coordination —
    they out-produced the Axis roughly three to one in aircraft and tanks — and on turning
    points at Stalingrad (February 1943), Midway (June 1942) and D-Day (6 June 1944). The war in
    Europe ended in May 1945; the Pacific in August, after atomic bombs destroyed Hiroshima (6
    August) and Nagasaki (9 August).
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="bet before you read on — which front decided it?" widget="predict" config={wwEasternPredict} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> the war in Europe was decided on the Western Front, D-Day and all. The Eastern Front was the war's centre of gravity: about four of every five German soldiers who died in the whole war died there, Stalingrad ended more than a year before the Normandy landings, and the Soviet Union paid for the victory with roughly 27 million dead. D-Day hastened the end; it did not decide it.</p>
  <p>
    At the centre of the war's meaning stands the <strong>Holocaust</strong>: the Nazi genocide
    of six million Jews, alongside Roma, disabled people, Slavic civilians, political prisoners
    and others. It was genocide industrialised — planned through state machinery (identification,
    expropriation, ghettos, then the killing centres of Operation Reinhard, Auschwitz-Birkenau
    and the Einsatzgruppen shootings in the east), requiring modern bureaucracy, railways and
    record-keeping. Historians still argue <em>intentionalism</em> (extermination was planned
    from the start) against <em>functionalism</em> (it escalated out of improvised wartime
    radicalisation); all agree on the warning in Himmler's 1943 boast that this "page of glory"
    would never be written: an atrocity planned in offices, executed by railway timetable, and
    hidden by ordinary administrative normality.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the second war was deadlier and more genuinely global than
    the first, its centre of gravity was the Eastern Front, and at the centre of its
    meaning stood a genocide planned in offices and executed by railway timetable.
  </p>

  <h2>What the wars built</h2>
  <p>
    The modern order is the wars' legacy, read as a checklist of reactions. The
    <strong>United Nations</strong> (1945) replaced the League with great-power vetoes — an
    institution designed around the failures of 1919. <strong>Welfare states</strong> spread
    because total war had already mobilised and rationed whole societies, and because soldiers
    returning expected something better than 1918's return. European <strong>empires dissolved</strong>:
    the wars had weakened the imperial powers, armed and trained colonial subjects, and made
    racial hierarchy ideologically indefensible — India was independent within two years of 1945,
    Africa mostly within fifteen. The <strong>Nuremberg trials</strong> established that "just
    following orders" is not a defence and invented the crime of crimes against humanity. And
    the <strong>atomic bomb</strong> split war itself in two: between states that have it,
    deterrence replaced war-fighting, and the superpower standoff that followed — the Cold War,
    the subject of the next lesson in this unit — began before the Second World War was fully
    over.
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="fill the blanks — the wars' legacy" widget="cloze" config={wwLegacyCloze} />

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>What two structural flaws made the alliance system a tripwire as well as a safety net?</li>
    <li>Why is Versailles described as punishing Germany "just enough to be enraged and not enough to be disabled"?</li>
    <li>What three cracks did the Second World War grow in?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) It turned any local quarrel into a potential
    continental one, and its secrecy meant no government could be sure what its partners
    would do — so each hedged with more arms. (2) Germany lost territory, its navy and
    its colonies, and accepted war guilt and reparations, yet was not dismembered or
    permanently occupied — enraged, but capable of seeking revenge. (3) Economic collapse
    from 1929, fascism in power in Italy and Germany, and appeasement — whose verdict of
    folly rests on what Munich taught Hitler.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The world wars are one extended crisis with an armistice in the middle: Versailles created the instabilities the Second grew in.</li>
    <li>The alliance system was a tripwire — local quarrels could escalate, and mobilisation timetables removed the last room for hesitation.</li>
    <li>Sarajevo set the timing; the preconditions — alliances, rivalries, arms races — set the probability.</li>
    <li>The First World War killed about 10 million soldiers and destroyed four empires; Versailles punished without disabling.</li>
    <li>The Second World War killed 60–80 million, most of them civilians; the Eastern Front was its centre of gravity.</li>
    <li>The Holocaust was genocide industrialised — planned through state machinery, executed by railway timetable.</li>
    <li>What the wars built: the UN, welfare states, decolonisation, Nuremberg's crimes against humanity, and the nuclear age.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    Frame 4 is the bridge: the superpower standoff that began before the Second World War
    was fully over is the subject of the next lesson, the Cold War. And the method met
    here — trigger, preconditions, contingency — is the apparatus the historical-method
    lesson introduced; you will use it on every international crisis this course asks
    about.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The causes debate is the deepest available lesson in how historians layer explanation. Fritz
    Fischer argued in the 1960s that Germany deliberately launched a war of conquest; the
    "sleepwalkers" school (Clark) answers that every capital share responsibility for sliding
    into a conflict none sought; between them sits the structural view that the alliance system,
    mobilisation plans and arms races made war probable while decisions made it actual. Notice
    the method: <em>trigger</em> (Sarajevo), <em>preconditions</em> (alliances, rivalries,
    timetables), <em>contingency</em> (the choices of July) — the same apparatus introduced in
    the historical-method lesson, applied to history's largest question. For the Second World
    War, the equivalent debate asks whether appeasement was folly or bought the democracies the
    year they needed to rearm; the discovery after 1945 of how close German rearmament was to
    collapse in 1938 has kept the argument alive.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two quantitative details repay attention. First, the demographic scar: France lost about
    10% of its active male population in 1914–18, and the interwar "hollow classes" shaped its
    defensiveness for a generation. Second, the production war: the USSR relocated over 1,500
    factories east of the Urals in 1941 and still out-built Germany — a lesson economists cite
    to this day about the limits of quality over quantity. The wars also seeded the sciences:
    radar, operational research, penicillin mass-production and computing (Colossus, ENIAC) all
    advanced under war funding, and the Manhattan Project created the model of big-state science
    that built the post-war university system. Catastrophe, it turns out, has a ledger; the task
    of a citizen is to read both columns.
  </p>
